WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.004
A TypeScript service may represent one
account as incoming JSON, a request object,

2
00:00:05.084 --> 00:00:09.074
a domain object, an ORM entity, a
persistence update, and an API response.

3
00:00:09.154 --> 00:00:12.822
Those representations have different
purposes and capabilities.

4
00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:15.347
Mass assignment, also called over-posting,

5
00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:19.609
occurs when externally supplied properties
cross too broadly from a request

6
00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:23.293
representation into a sensitive model or
persistence operation.

7
00:00:23.373 --> 00:00:27.813
The statement that updates an account with
data equal to req.body is deliberately

8
00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:28.343
unsafe.

9
00:00:28.423 --> 00:00:30.868
The problem is not unique to a particular
ORM.

10
00:00:30.948 --> 00:00:35.324
Object spread, Object.assign, entity
merging, document-database operators,

11
00:00:35.404 --> 00:00:38.171
automatic model binding, generic PATCH
handlers,

12
00:00:38.251 --> 00:00:41.275
and nested writes can create the same
boundary failure.

13
00:00:41.355 --> 00:00:45.666
The persistence tool normally performs the
update the application requested;

14
00:00:45.746 --> 00:00:49.157
it does not know which fields that
endpoint intended to expose.

15
00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:53.934
Create and upsert endpoints need the same
discipline because server-owned defaults,

16
00:00:54.014 --> 00:00:58.132
identifiers, and relationships can be
over-posted before a record exists.

17
00:00:58.212 --> 00:01:01.751
A client can submit fields that the
visible form never contained.

18
00:01:01.831 --> 00:01:05.435
Role, tenant identifier, owner identifier,
verification state,

19
00:01:05.515 --> 00:01:09.182
billing status, account balance, approval
flags, audit metadata,

20
00:01:09.262 --> 00:01:12.094
and workflow state may all exist in the
stored model.

21
00:01:12.174 --> 00:01:15.777
Hiding or disabling those controls in the
UI is not authorization.

22
00:01:15.857 --> 00:01:17.981
Authentication is also insufficient:

23
00:01:18.061 --> 00:01:21.986
a user may be allowed to edit a profile
but not to change administrative or

24
00:01:22.066 --> 00:01:24.253
server-owned state on that same record.

25
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:27.551
The server must define writable fields for
each operation.

26
00:01:27.631 --> 00:01:31.234
Parse and validate the request against a
narrow runtime contract,

27
00:01:31.314 --> 00:01:34.725
authorize both the target object and the
requested operation,

28
00:01:34.805 --> 00:01:39.566
apply business rules, and construct the
persistence update from approved properties.

29
00:01:39.646 --> 00:01:44.344
New database columns should remain
unreachable until an operation deliberately maps

30
00:01:44.424 --> 00:01:44.745
them.

31
00:01:44.825 --> 00:01:49.201
The durable distinction is that a request
body describes what the client wants to

32
00:01:49.281 --> 00:01:52.820
change; it does not define what the client
is permitted to change.
